This is a more powerful theorem than it may appear at first, because although
the statement of the theorem is `a = a`, Lean will allow anything that is
definitionally equal to that type. So, for instance, `2 + 2 = 4` is proven in
Lean by `rfl`, because both sides are the same up to definitional equality.
-/
@[match_pattern] def rfl {α : Sort u} {a : α} : Eq a a := Eq.refl a
</ITEM>
<ITEM>
--name=Finset.mem_singleton
--context_item_type=theorem
@[simp]
theorem mem_singleton {a b : α} : b ∈ ({a} : Finset α) ↔ b = a :=
  Multiset.mem_singleton
</ITEM>
<ITEM>
--name=Finset.mem_insert
--context_item_type=theorem
@[simp]
theorem mem_insert : a ∈ insert b s ↔ a = b ∨ a ∈ s :=
  mem_ndinsert
</ITEM>
<ITEM>
--name=Finset.card_singleton
--context_item_type=theorem
@[simp]
theorem card_singleton (a : α) : #{a} = 1 :=
  Multiset.card_singleton _
</ITEM>
<ITEM>
--name=Finset.card_insert_of_not_mem
--context_item_type=theorem
@[simp]
theorem card_insert_of_not_mem (h : a ∉ s) : #(insert a s) = #s + 1 := by
  rw [← cons_eq_insert _ _ h, card_cons]
